-- title:
--   Smoothness of a scheme representing relative Pic⁰
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a Noetherian commutative ring and let $c \colon C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension one and geometrically integral, and let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $c$, that is, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to C$ with $\varepsilon$ followed by $c$ equal to the identity. Assume that for every $m_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ there is a datum $\mathfrak{F}$ of type `SmoothProperCurve.FiniteMapData` for $c$ and $\varepsilon$ with $m_0 \le \mathfrak{F}.m$: a pair of affine opens $U, V$ covering $C$, with $U$ the complement of the image of $\varepsilon$, sections $f \in \Gamma(C,U)$ and $g \in \Gamma(C,V)$ whose basic open sets are both $U \cap V$ and whose restrictions to $U \cap V$ are mutually inverse, such that $R[X] \to \Gamma(C,U)$, $X \mapsto f$, and $R[X] \to \Gamma(C,V)$, $X \mapsto g$, are finite, and such that for every local $R$-algebra $S$ and every $s \in S$ the quotient $S \otimes_R \Gamma(C,U)$ by the ideal generated by $1 \otimes f - s \otimes 1$ is a finite free $S$-module of rank $\mathfrak{F}.m$. Let $D$ consist of a scheme $D.P$, a structure morphism $D.toBase \colon D.P \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ and a section $D.zeroSection$ of it, and suppose $D$ represents the subfunctor of the rigidified relative Picard functor cut out by `algEquivZeroCut c ε`: there is a rigidified line bundle (an invertible module on $C \times_R D.P$, trivialised along the induced rigidifying section) whose restriction to the fibre above every point $\operatorname{Spec} k \to D.P$ with $k$ algebraically closed satisfies `IsAlgEquivZero`; for every $t \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ and every rigidified line bundle $M$ on $C \times_R T$ with the same fibrewise property there is a unique morphism $T \to D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R$ along which the pullback of the Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to $M$; and the pullback along $D.zeroSection$ is isomorphic to the unit bundle. If moreover $D.toBase$ is locally of finite type, then $D.toBase$ is smooth.
--
--   This is the smoothness of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of a smooth proper curve with geometrically integral fibres, in the form: any pointed $R$-scheme, locally of finite type, representing the algebraic-equivalence-to-zero part of the rigidified relative Picard functor is smooth over the base; here the curve is assumed to carry finite maps to the projective line of arbitrarily large degree with pole only along the section, recorded chart-wise. It feeds the construction of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ over a reduced base, where such a representing scheme is produced together with its smoothness.
